    "body": "This PR introduces support for following syntax:\r\n```python\r\ntype ListOfInts = list[int]\r\n\r\nA: ListOfInts = [1,2,3]\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Currently works:\r\n* parser (not sure if done right, in python it is soft keyword [1]\r\n* Simple usage with tests\r\n* Using aliases inside type subscriptions\r\n* Composite usage (type alias having type aliases inside)\r\n* C tuple support\r\n* some tests\r\n\r\n## Pending:\r\n* Code is written in a dirty way. I suppose at least `TypeAliasType` needs to be implemented in different way, not sure how is the best way, definitely not how it is currently implemented. Also not as Builtin type too.\r\n* warnings when alias is used that does not exist: `type new_alias = list[BadAlias]`\r\n* using and assigning type alias - e.g. `print(TypeAlias)` crashes cython\r\n* More tests of different combinations - alias of struct, etc...\r\n\r\n## Python compatibility\r\n* `type` keyword was introduced in python 3.12.\r\n* Older mechanisms are:\r\n  - simple assignment: `TypeAlias = list[int]`. I would prefer to avoid this.\r\n  - type annotated assignment introduced in python 3.10:\r\n    ```\r\n    from typing import TypeAlias\r\n    Vector: TypeAlias = list[float]\r\n    ```\r\n    I would consider support of this syntax in next PR...\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> Not intended for Cython 3.3 unless a miracle happens. \r\n\r\n[1] https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#soft-keywords",
